  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe Northern Plains lies under a broad upper trough, in zonal to \nslight northwest flow, extending from a large closed low hovering \nover the Hudson Bay as a ridge (and its mid level high\nunderneath) continues over the western CONUS. Through the end \nof the week and into the start of the weekend, this low will \npretty much wobble over the Hudson Bay area (and retrograding a \nbit) before finally weakening and tracking southeast by the end \nof the weekend into early next week. With this, several embedded\nshortwaves/vort maxes will spin cyclonically around the low \nacross the Northern Plains into Canada within this ongoing west \nto northwest flow aloft. Behind the upper low, the ridge out \nwest weakens and becomes more broad, with the axis still through\nthe western CONUS with the Northern Plains on the downstream \nside of it through the middle part of next week as there is not \nmuch movement in the pattern. At the surface, the high pressure \nsystem that has been over the region will drift off towards the \nsoutheast through this evening with winds at the surface that \nbecoming more southerly tonight/overnight. A surface trough \nsets up over west central SD Thursday into Thursday evening with\noverall southerly flow continuing before a weak cold front \nmoves through early Friday, switching winds out of the north to \nnortheast. By the weekend, the front lifts back northward as a \nstationary front as low pressure develops over western SD \nthrough MT.\n\nEmbedded shortwave energy over NE through northeastern SD aloft\nThursday evening/Friday morning will interact with the surface \ntrough and cold front to provide weak energy/lift. To the east \nof the trough, WAA flow at 850mb/surface will aid in dewpoints \nrising into the 60s by the afternoon, along with warm temps, \nhelping the atmosphere to destabilize creating the potential for\nshowers and thunderstorms as a weak LLJ develops through the \nnight. NBM highlights pops of 15-25% starting over NE/south \ncentral SD Thursday evening and tracking east combining with \nanother potential area of convection (pops of 30-45%) tracking \nsoutheast out of ND/MN with much of the convection mainly east \nof the James River (associated with the cold front and help from\nLLJ) through early Friday morning. However, CAMs tell a \ndifferent story and really do not show much in the way of any \nconvection firing up with Fv3 being the only model to show \nsomething as CAMs handle weak forcing poorly. Also, drier air \nwill be in place over much of the region. Therefore, as of now \nconfidence remains low at this time. So if cells can initiate, \nthere will be marginal instability to work as HREF indicates up \nto 1500 j/kg of mean SB/MUCAPE, highest over south central \nthrough northeastern SD with max potential to around 2500j/kg \nhere, and bulk shear around 30kts (enough to produce a couple\nsupercells). Instability does decrease after sunset, however \nbulk shear/LLJ, will be ongoing. The SPC has highlighted a Day 2\nmarginal risk (level 1/5) for isolated severe storms that \ncovers the southern half of Jones and Lyman Counties and \nsouthward where this better instability+shear lies. Main threat\nwill be 1\" diameter hail and wind gusts of 60 mph. Other then \nslight pops (15-20%) over south central SD Saturday morning, the\nforecast remains dry through the day Sunday. Our next potential\nfor widespread chances of rain (pops 35-65%) will not be until \nSunday evening into Monday morning per stronger northwest flow \naloft, per another shortwave, coinciding with an area of low \npressure/frontal boundary and warm and moist air at the surface.\nStill a bit early on severe potential with ML guidance \nindicating a 5% chance of severe weather over the Northern \nPlains.\n\nWith southerly flow returning, overnight temps will be warmer than \nthe previous 2 nights, ranging in the upper 50s to the lower 60s and \nhighs back into the upper 80s to upper 90s west of the James River \nfor Thursday. Behind the cold front Friday, highs will range in the \nupper 70s to the upper 80s, warmest across central SD. Temps will \nwarm back up for the weekend, ranging in the lower 80s to the lower \n90s, again warmest across central SD. As for the smoke, light \nconcentrations of surface smoke are possible over central SD this \nevening and tonight before lifting northward. This should not reduce \nvisibility at the surface. Smoke aloft continues with widespread, \nthicker smoke moving in over the entire region Thursday night and \nFriday behind the cold front. Lastly, with southwest flow gusty \ndownslope winds off the Coteau are possible both early Thursday and \nFriday morning with gusts of 35 to potentially to 40 mph.",
